# Dome consumer workflow

These instructions and templates match Dome {{.Version}}.

Before authoring, compare `go list -m -f '{{"{{.Version}}"}}' github.com/jani123/dome`
with `dome.json` fields `dome_version`, `skill_version`, and `template_version`.
If versions differ, stop API-dependent changes: run the initializer at the pinned
dependency version into a temporary project, review the skill/template differences,
and synchronize metadata/assets with the dependency. Do not overwrite custom Go
or instructions. Local replacements require checking the replacement source too.

Use `go doc` and source from `go list -m -f '{{"{{.Dir}}"}}' github.com/jani123/dome`
for the installed API; use version-matched examples and docs. Do not invent APIs
from current-main documentation for an older dependency.

Define topology in Go, register modules explicitly, and decode values into typed
YAML structs. Use SOPS for encrypted secrets. Resource IDs must remain stable;
never persist secret contents. Snapshots should contain hashes/non-secret data.

Build with `CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o agent ./cmd/agent`, run `go test ./...`, then
inspect `./agent plan --json` from the project root. Check updates and pruning
against the user's request. Apply only when requested, using `./agent apply --json`.
The compiled agent runs independently of Codex; no model calls belong in runtime.

For playbooks, inspect `go doc github.com/jani123/dome/core/playbook`. Every step
needs Observe/Apply/Verify plus safe repeatability or explicit Recover. Declare
dependencies and resource locks; bump the playbook Version when callbacks change.
For custom modules, implement the public resource contracts and restore deletion
handles without old secret values. Observe actual host state, verify before
recording applied state, and prune only previously managed resources.

For failures use `./agent operations list`, `operations inspect <id>`, and
`playbook run <name> --resume <id>` as appropriate. Re-observe interrupted work;
never bypass an unfinished transition by changing/removing its target or deleting
SQLite state. Failed dependencies stay blocked. Keep the last successful Git
revision on failures. Inspect the exact installed API before adding recovery code.

Hand over source, dependency/build material, and configuration with customer
binaries; preserve the customer's ability to rebuild and change providers.

For a development consumer, keep the resolved Go pseudo-version in the dependency
and every asset version field. Never replace that immutable pin with `main`.
Use local `go doc` for the pinned API; next guides may describe newer main source.
